%I A400659 #14 Oct 08 2026 18:49:38
%S A400659 2,1,1,3,1,1,2,5,5,4,16,4,1,12,9,2,5,3,6,2,34,3,2,1,6,65,3,5,6,3,229,
%T A400659 11,17,5,7,16,19,15,46,11,14,20,10,207,12,12,32,63,6,93,4,16,96,5,5,
%U A400659 329,172,34,68,4,395,150,20,2,20,1,54,1,106,5,103,11
%N A400659 Smallest exponent k >= 1 such that (product of the first n-1 primes) * prime(n)^k - 1 is prime; or -1 is no such prime exists.
%C A400659 Let Y(n,k) = A002110(n-1)*prime(n)^k - 1 for n, k >= 1. Row 1 of Y is the Mersenne numbers 2^k - 1, and column 1 is A002110(n) - 1.
%C A400659 a(n) is the position of the first prime in row n of Y.
%C A400659 a(n) = 1 if and only if n is in A057704.
%C A400659 a(1) = 2 because Y(1,1) = 1 is not prime and Y(1,2) = 3 is prime.
%C A400659 It is not known that a(n) exists for every n.
%H A400659 Yagel Bar, <a href="/A400659/b400659.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..100</a>.
%H A400659 Yagel Bar, <a href="https://yagelnumbers.org/paper/live/">Yagel Numbers: the Mersenne x Primorial Grid</a>.
%e A400659 For n = 4, the 4th prime is 7, and the previous three primes are 2, 3, 5, whose product is 30. So:
%e A400659 k = 1: 30*7 - 1 = 209 = 11*19, composite.
%e A400659 k = 2: 30*7^2 - 1 = 1469 = 13*113, composite.
%e A400659 k = 3: 30*7^3 - 1 = 10289, which is prime.
%e A400659 So a(4) = 3.
%o A400659 (PARI) a(n) = my(h=prod(j=1,n-1,prime(j)), p=prime(n), k=1); while(!isprime(h*p^k-1), k++); k /* Slow for large n */
%Y A400659 Cf. A000040, A002110, A000043, A003307, A057704, A006794.
%K A400659 nonn,hard,new
%O A400659 1,1
%A A400659 _Yagel Bar_, Oct 02 2026
